Both of these descriptions in the different narrations are authentic as they are found in Bukhari & Muslim; the hadith for the 2nd Messiah is considered to be a golden chain, the most reliable Isnad (transmission of a report) in the science of Hadith. All the versions in Bukhari & Muslim which describe the appearance of Jesus mention him with (أَحْمَرُ) red complexion with (جَعْدٌ) curly hair expect the ones that describe the Latter Day Messiah which mention him with (آدَمُ) brown complexion with (سَبْطٌ) straight hair. A mistake is not repeated so many different times and is recorded in so many different places in the 2 most authentic books after the Holy Quran.

The Mosaic Messiah who passed away (أَحْمَرُ red complexion with جَعْدٌ curly hair) and is always seen with the those who passed away while the Islamic Messiah who was to come after him (آدَمُ brown complexion with سَبْطٌ straight hair) is always seen with the those who came after him.

ARGUMENT 1 – DIFFERENT APPEARANCE

From the 2 different descriptions it can be established that:

•
The 1st Messiah was not آدَمُ (brown) complexion in contrast to Moses who was, rather the 1st Messiah was أَحْمَرُ (red) complexion. The 1st Messiah was not long سَبْطٌ (straight haired) in contrast to Moses who was, rather the 1st Messiah was جَعْدٌ (curly haired).

•
The 2nd Messiah was not أَحْمَرُ (red) complexion in contrast to the Dajjal who was, rather the 2nd Messiah was آدَمُ (brown) complexion. The 2nd Messiah was not جَعْدٌ (curly haired) in contrast to the Dajjal who was, rather the 2nd Messiah was long سَبْطٌ (straight haired).

•
In another version Ibn Umar acknowledged that some people said that Jesus was أَحْمَرُ (red) complexion, but he swears by Allah that Jesus was of آدَمُ (brown) complexion. This means آدَمُ and أَحْمَرُ are not the same as a companion would not have taken an oath by Allah on this, which is a very significant thing that would rarely occur!

If there was only 1 singular Messiah, then Muhammad ﷺ wouldn’t give us 2 different descriptions which are diametrically opposed to one another! Nor would not recognize the same person!

ARGUMENT 2 – FAILURE TO RECOGNISE

The visions in which Muhammad ﷺ observed the two Messiahs were in fact Divine Revelation, meaning there was no room for error in regard to what the Prophet ﷺ was witnessing! However, we see in ahadith that on two different occasions Muhammad ﷺ is asking for the identity of the person who is referred to as the “Messiah”, one time by Jibrail the other time by the people:

•
In Mi’raj that happened in 5th year of his prophethood at the time when the Holy Prophet ﷺ was sleeping in Ka`aba, seen with the passed away Prophets.

•
In a dream of the Holy Prophet ﷺ when the Holy Prophet ﷺ was sleeping in Ka`aba, seen with Dajjal.

If the Holy Prophet Muhammad ﷺ had already met the Messiah, then why would he feel the need to ask the identity of the very same person who he had already seen previously. However, we see from ahadith that this indeed did occur, leaving us with two possibilities:

•
God Forbid, did the Holy Prophet ﷺ forget the description of Isa(as) after seeing him in one of the most spiritual experiences in his life?

•
The Messiah was a totally different individual then the one he ﷺ saw previously and hence the Prophet ﷺ felt the need to enquire about him as his appearance did not resemble anyone who he ﷺ had seen prior.

If there was only 1 singular Messiah, then Muhammad ﷺ would not be asking for the identity of the very same person who he had seen previously! Nor would he provide a completely different description!

REFUTATIONS

Some people raise the objection that the Messiah who would descend would be of أَحْمَرُ (red) complexion citing this hadith:

Narrated Abu Hurayrah:
The Prophet (ﷺ) said: There is no prophet between me and him, that is, Jesus (ﷺ). He will descent. When you see him, recognise him: a man of medium height, (حْمَرُ) reddish fair, wearing two light yellow garments, looking as if drops were falling down from his head though it will not be wet. He will fight the people for the cause of Islam. He will break the cross, kill swine, and abolish jizyah. Allah will perish all religions except Islam. He will destroy the Antichrist and will live on the earth for forty years and then he will die. The Muslims will pray over him.
[Sunan Abi Dawud 4324]

Even if this hadith can be accepted on account of Riwayat (Narration) the aspect of the hadith that talks about the Messiah to come being أَحْمَرُ (red) complexion should be rejected on account of Dirayat (Rationalization) as the narration should not be contradictory to any narration of greater authenticity. The narrations by Ibn Umar about the physical descriptions of the Messiah son of Mary & Dajjal are supported by a golden chain and are of a much higher order than this narration, hence they are found in Bukhari or Muslim while this narration isn’t. In addition to this, it shows that there was confusion among the sahaba regarding the appearance of the Messiah due to the fact that the Holy Prophet had given 2 distinct descriptions. Hence Ibn Umar (ra) felt the need to clarify that the latter day Messiah would not be of أَحْمَرُ (red) complexion but rather of a آدَمُ (brown) complexion (Sahih al-Bukhari 3441, Book 60 Prophets).

Imam al-Nawawi also considered the word أَحْمَرُ (red) to possibly be a mistake of the narrator.

CONCLUSION

Many people raise the objection that Jesus (as) must still be alive in the heavens and would return to the Earth one day, as the ahadith mentioned the descend of a person referred to as the Messiah son of Mary. However, it is clear from Quran and Hadith that the Messiah of the Children of Israel has passed away a natural death. Furthermore, as established from the words of Muhammad ﷺ, the Messiah who would descend in the latter days is not the same as the one that was sent to the children of Israel. The Messiah of the Children of Israel being described as having أَحْمَرُ (red) complexion with جَعْدٌ (curly hair), while the Messiah of the Latter Days has آدَمُ (brown) complexion with long سَبْطٌ (straight) hair. In this article we will analyse the two different descriptions from the ahadith. So, if indeed Jesus was raised to the heavens with his physical body, he will never descend as the one to come is definitely not the same Messiah sent to the children of Israel. This explains that the hadith about the descent of the Messiah do not contradict the qur’anic verdict that Jesus had passed away a natural death and will not return, his grave will be on earth, and he will not have everlasting life in heaven. The two Messiahs would be different people, they would share the same Messianic title.

